{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"matrix matrix","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md","fileName":"Matrix.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Matrix","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Matrix and Vector Computation]] · [[Matrix Algebra for Statistical Models]] · [[FEM Mathematical Foundations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Matrix and Vector Computation","Matrix Algebra for Statistical Models","FEM Mathematical Foundations","Matrix Multiplication","Vector","Matrix Addition","Scalar Multiplication of a Matrix","Matrix Transpose","Square Matrix","Determinant","Matrix Inverse","Rectangular Matrix","algorithms.epub","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf","finiteelementanalysis_aprimer.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"matrix matrix","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md","fileName":"Matrix.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Matrix","heading":"Matrix","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of values organized into horizontal rows and vertical columns. Each position is identified by its row and column, allowing related quantities and transformations to be expressed compactly.\n\nMatrices can represent graph connections, sales data, neural-network parameters, or other structured numerical relationships. [[Matrix Multiplication]] combines compatible matrices, while a [[Vector]] can be treated as the special case of a matrix with one row or one column.\n\nMatrix algebra also includes [[Matrix Addition]], [[Scalar Multiplication of a Matrix]], and [[Matrix Transpose]]. Shape and structure matter: a [[Square Matrix]] may have a [[Determinant]], [[Matrix Inverse]], eigenvalues, and factorizations, while a [[Rectangular Matrix]] naturally represents overdetermined or underdetermined systems.\n\nFor statistical modeling, a matrix can place observations in rows and model terms in columns so one equation represents an entire dataset. This representation makes the relationship among a design, its coefficients, fitted values, and residuals explicit.\n\nFinite element formulations use matrix notation to collect element coefficients, nodal unknowns, and loads. Assembly ultimately produces a large simultaneous system in which rows and columns follow the ordering of global degrees of freedom.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Matrix and Vector Computation","Matrix Algebra for Statistical Models","FEM Mathematical Foundations","Matrix Multiplication","Vector","Matrix Addition","Scalar Multiplication of a Matrix","Matrix Transpose","Square Matrix","Determinant","Matrix Inverse","Rectangular Matrix","algorithms.epub","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf","finiteelementanalysis_aprimer.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"matrix matrix","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Matrix.md","fileName":"Matrix.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Matrix","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algorithms.epub]]\n\n[[appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf]]\n\n[[dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf]]\n\n[[finiteelementanalysis_aprimer.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Matrix and Vector Computation","Matrix Algebra for Statistical Models","FEM Mathematical Foundations","Matrix Multiplication","Vector","Matrix Addition","Scalar Multiplication of a Matrix","Matrix Transpose","Squ